R&C patch 7061: template prewarm jobs run in the async PSO queue's lowest class (load stalls, the RE notes s.30).
KZ3 2071's prewarm ran its own threads (LD_PSO_TEMPLATE_THREADS, default 3) beside the 7052 workers. In ToD's cold
level load most of the template's programs only appear in guest memory with the level (fragment programs 18/87 found
before it, 66/87 at 239 s), so its builds land in the same seconds as the walker's demand and lookahead builds and
compete with them: tmpl-on-cold (2026-11-11) built 292 template pipelines, the walker used 26, and the first
Metropolis frame took 16.6 s against 13.0 s for tmpl-off-cold on the same exe.
Now each template record is a job of the 7052 queue (rsx_pso_prio.h) in the lowest class: priority RSX_APQ_TEMPLATE,
after every lookahead build and every demand, never promoted, owning no shared compile of the queue. A worker takes one
only when no build is queued, and at most min(LD_PSO_TEMPLATE_THREADS or 3, workers - 1) run at once, so a worker is
always free for the next demand (one worker, LD_PSO_ASYNC_THREADS=1 only: cap 0, 2071's own threads instead). A pop declined at that cap spends the worker's semaphore count; a finished template
job releases one while templates are still queued. n_queued (the lookahead backlog cap, the [pso-await] queue column)
counts builds only. The queue starts once from whichever comes first, the walker's first submission or the prewarm's
start (InitOnce). LD_PSO_TEMPLATE_QUEUE=0 or LD_PSO_ASYNC=0 keeps 2071's own threads.
Host test port/rc/tests/run_pso_prio_test.sh section 6: templates queued first still come after the lookahead builds
and the demand; the cap; a build is taken at the cap; fuzz with a semaphore model (no template taken while a build is
queued, never above the cap, no template stranded). Mutants (class ignored, no cap, no release) each fail it.
2026-11-11 (code review of rc-int-1600): nothing joined a running template job any more, so at exit one could still be in
ld_pl_create when ld_pl_shutdown released the pipeline library and device (the 2048 build jobs had the same gap):
ld_apso_quiesce now stops the workers taking jobs and waits for the running ones (builds and templates) before
ld_pt_shutdown / ld_pl_shutdown, at most 10 s (the library is kept if one still runs). And the cap clamped one worker up
to 1, so a template job could hold the only worker; it is 0 now and the prewarm keeps 2071's threads in that case.

